@page! RoverLab
@section Appendix · RoverLab: Build, Connect, Train
@label Appendix · Our game
@plane none

**RoverLab** is the module's interactive game. You build a small machine from real-world parts, connect it to a network, teach it how to respond to what it senses, and send it on a mission. A first experiment takes about **5–10 minutes**, needs no experience, and runs in any modern browser — on a computer, tablet or phone.

@image roverlab-home.jpg w=84 center | The RoverLab start screen. Choose **Start new mission** to open your workbench.

@row
@box dark | Play it now
Open **[roverlab-blue-impulse.vercel.app](https://roverlab-blue-impulse.vercel.app)** or scan the code. Your progress is saved in your browser, and after the first visit the game keeps working offline.

The complete source can also be downloaded from the project's GitHub page and played without an internet connection.
@end
@image qr-roverlab.png w=30 | Scan to play
@endrow

@page Three machines, three missions
@label Appendix · Our game
@plane none

RoverLab is one game with three machines. Each machine has its own mission, so the same ideas — sensing, connecting and acting — are tested in three different situations.

@box light | Choose your machine
| Machine | Mission | What it must do |
|---|---|---|
| **Exploration rover** | Explore | Cross rough terrain, avoid obstacles, take a reading at the research site, send it back and return home. |
| **Service robot** | Search | Find people, move safely around them, report them, and return before the battery runs low. |
| **Industrial machine** | Monitor | Watch a worksite's temperature, stop and report when it gets too hot, and restart when it is safe. |
@end

@image roverlab-machine.jpg w=60 center | Step 1 of 7: every experiment starts by choosing a machine.

@box alt | The seven steps of an experiment
@flow 1 Machine > 2 Hardware > 3 Network > 4 Training > 5 Mission > 6 Simulation > 7 Results
You can return to any earlier step, change one decision and run the mission again to see what difference it makes.
@end

@page Build and connect
@label Appendix · Our game
@plane none

@row
@image roverlab-hardware.jpg w=56 | Step 2 — Hardware: add parts from the component library; the pre-flight check says when the build is ready.
@box dark | Build · Chapter 3
Every part has a job. **Sensors** detect the world, the **processor** chooses a response, **motors** carry it out and the **battery** limits how much you can add. Inspect a part to read what it does; remove it and watch which abilities disappear.
@end
@endrow

@row
@box light | Connect · Chapter 4
Pick a communication module — Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, radio, Ethernet or cellular — link your robot to a node such as the base station, then **test the network**. A link that works at the base can still fail far away in the field: range matters.
@end
@image roverlab-network.jpg w=56 | Step 3 — Network: choose a module, connect two nodes and test the link.
@endrow

@box alt w=84 right | Why the order matters
Hardware comes first because a robot cannot sense what it has no sensor for, and cannot report what it has no network for. The game checks each step before it lets you continue.
@end

@page Train, run and read the results
@label Appendix · Our game
@plane none

@row
@image roverlab-training.jpg w=51 | Step 4 — Training: write IF–THEN rules, test a stimulus and reward a useful response.
@box dark | Train · Chapter 2
Write at least three **IF stimulus → THEN response** rules. Test a stimulus and give feedback: **useful response** strengthens the association, **needs work** weakens it. Stronger rules win.
@end
@endrow

@row
@box light | Run · Chapter 5
Launch the mission and watch it play out: pause, step, restart or speed up. The **event log** traces each sensor reading to the rule, action and message it caused.
@end
@image roverlab-simulation.jpg w=51 | Step 6 — Simulation: your robot out in the world.
@endrow

@row
@image roverlab-results.jpg w=51 | Step 7 — Results: four scores and the evidence behind them.
@box alt | Reflect · Chapter 6
The report scores **hardware, network, behaviour and mission completion** and explains each one. A high score in a simplified world is a good start — not proof that a real robot would be safe.
@end
@endrow

@page RoverLab and this guide
@label Appendix · Our game
@plane climb br

@box light | Learning connections
| Chapter | What to investigate in RoverLab |
|---|---|
| 2 · Behaviorism & Behaviorist AI | Stimulus–response associations; feedback changes how strongly competing rules are preferred. |
| 3 · Hardware | Sensors, processors, memory, power and actuators each have a different job. |
| 4 · Networking | Endpoints, routes, range and what happens when communication fails. |
| 5 · Applications | Readiness checks, practical constraints and how a whole system fits together. |
| 6 · Strengths & Limitations | Why success in a simplified simulation does not prove real-world safety. |
@end

@box dark w=90 | An experiment to try
1. Build the exploration rover with the starter parts and complete the mission.
2. Remove one sensor and run the mission again. Which objective failed, and why?
3. Change to a short-range network module. Does the reading still reach the base?
4. Swap the order of two rules. Which situations now produce a different action?
@end

@box alt w=86 right | What the game is — and is not
RoverLab is a **teaching model**. You write the rules, and feedback only changes how strongly each rule is preferred — it is not a neural network or a full reinforcement-learning algorithm. Power, range and timing values are simplified, and every mission is deterministic, so the same set-up always gives the same result. Change one thing at a time and compare.
@end
